---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Agent Plugins compatibility"
  description="The Agent Plugins v1.0.0 compatibility target, current implementation status, and safety requirements for Veasel Code."
>
  <section class="doc-page">
    <p class="kicker">EXTENSIBILITY / COMPATIBILITY</p>
    <h1>
      One portable format.
      <br />
      <em>A careful host.</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Veasel Code targets the released Agent Plugins v1.0.0 specification. A
      compatible host must understand packages, Skills, and MCP configuration,
      then add its own clear trust and permission controls.
    </p>
    <div class="compatibility-banner">
      <span class="compatibility-mark" aria-hidden="true">
        ◌
      </span>
      <div>
        <strong>
          Experimental Skills and MCP support · no compatibility claim yet
        </strong>
        <p>
          Veasel discovers local packages, can activate validated Skills per
          session, and can expose trusted stdio or Streamable HTTP MCP servers
          as model tools. Active Skills can expose bounded UTF-8 resources from
          their own directory for on-demand reading. Experimental
          <code>allowed-tools</code> declarations restrict the exact tool names
          available to the session; they cannot add tools or bypass approvals.
          Skill text and resource files are untrusted, and scripts are not run
          automatically. Veasel does not install packages or claim full v1.0.0
          conformance.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What the format brings</h2>
      <div class="guide-grid">
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">{}</span>
          <h3>Plugin package</h3>
          <p>
            A portable directory starts with a versioned{" "}
            <code>plugin.json</code> manifest.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">✳</span>
          <h3>Agent Skills</h3>
          <p>
            Discover descriptions first, activate instructions per session, and
            read bounded text resources from that Skill when needed.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="guide-card">
          <span class="guide-card-mark">⌘</span>
          <h3>MCP servers</h3>
          <p>
            Declare stdio or Streamable HTTP connections in a package’s{" "}
            <code>mcp.json</code>.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Try session Skills and MCP servers</h2>
      <p>
        Place a package directory containing <code>plugin.json</code> under
        <code>$VEASEL_DATA_DIR/plugins</code>, or set
        <code>VEASEL_PLUGIN_DIR</code> to another directory. Start the local
        backend and open a session in the TUI.
      </p>
      <pre><code>/skills
/skill on review-tools/review
/skill off review-tools/review
/mcp
/mcp trust review-tools/local
/mcp untrust review-tools/local</code></pre>
      <p>
        The plugin and Skill names come from the validated package manifest and
        directory. Skill selection and MCP trust persist with the session.
        Disabling a Skill removes its instructions from the next model turn. MCP
        tools from trusted servers are available to the model, but every
        requested call pauses for review. Use <code>/mcp calls</code>, inspect
        one with <code>/mcp call show &lt;id&gt;</code>, then approve or reject
        it. Rejected calls are not retried. While a Skill is active, the model
        may read UTF-8 files only from that Skill’s directory; path traversal
        and symlink escapes are rejected. Script files are never executed
        automatically. A declared <code>allowed-tools</code> list restricts
        exact Veasel tool names already available to the session; it cannot
        grant MCP trust or bypass file and shell approvals. When multiple active
        Skills declare the field, their tool names combine. An empty list denies
        all tools; a missing declaration adds no restriction. Skill instructions
        and resources remain untrusted input.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-panel">
      <h2>What remains before a conformance claim</h2>
      <ul class="guide-list">
        <li>
          Managed installation and updates, a registry of source and version,
          component-level enablement, and persistent per-install data.
        </li>
        <li>
          The complete spec-derived fixture matrix, Windows junction behavior,
          additional transport lifecycle cases, and the documented
          <code>com.veasel.code</code> extension contract.
        </li>
        <li>
          Binary Skill assets and Skill-script execution with explicit approval
          are not supported. MCP stdio processes still run with the current
          user’s OS privileges and without an OS sandbox (MCP remains
          unsandboxed; approved shell may wrap separately).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <aside class="callout">
      <strong>Why compatibility is more than parsing.</strong> A plugin may
      start a local process or send data to a remote endpoint. Veasel must make
      those effects visible and keep them inside user-approved boundaries.
    </aside>
    <p class="doc-next">
      <a class="text-link" href="https://agent-plugins.org/specification">
        Read the Agent Plugins specification <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/veasel-labs/veasel/blob/main/docs/compatibility/agent-plugins.md"
      >
        Review the conformance matrix <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href={`${basePath}/docs/security/`}>
        See the security boundary <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
